## Live `_slots` landing latency and the design traceability table
================================================================================

Context: the microbatch fast path reads `_slots` directly, so the KPI that
matters for it is the time from a source commit to the slot landing in
`_slots`, not to the `_graph` view. A second continuous run of the change-feed
slot pipeline on one fixture table (scratch schema, dropped afterwards) measured
it; the traceability table was restructured so "built as designed" and "verified
on the workspace" are two separate facts per row.

Changes:

1. docs/incremental-materialization.md — Live verification: new bullet with the
   `_slots` landing latency; Design traceability: `Built` and `Live` columns
   (✅ / ◐ / ⛔ and ✅ / ○ / —), every row annotated with what the workspace
   exercised. Rows that were ◐ for a justified reading of the design are ✅ with
   the reason kept in `Where`: `_inferred` reset (a MERGE skip changes nothing,
   so it keeps the inferences), `_graph` as an MV on the Lakehouse engine only
   (the design wants the MV for Lakehouse//RT reads, and only that engine's
   readers read `_graph`), Lakebase syncing from `_data` (the design offers
   `_slots` or `_data`), and the realtime processing/operations rows (built;
   live column ○). The KPI row carries every live number.
2. changelogs/v0.9.2/dermot-smyth_2026-11-02.log — this section.

Measured (2026-11-02, continuous pipeline, eight single-row source updates, the
slots table polled every second from the build warehouse):
- source commit → new label in `_slots`: 8.1 s median, 11.9 s max, 3.1 s min
  (n = 8; samples 11.9, 8.1, 8.0, 8.1, 8.2, 10.3, 3.1, 8.2). A Lakehouse//RT
  read adds query latency only, so the design's ≤ 30 s p95 holds at the
  pipeline's cadence; the RT read itself is unmeasured (RT is enabled on the
  workspace but an RT warehouse is created in the UI only, and none exists).

