## Live run of the realtime processing leg on Spark (serverless notebook, AvailableNow)
================================================================================

Context: the Phase 3 processor (`SlotDiffStatefulProcessor` under
`transformWithState`) and `LiveSink` had only been exercised without Spark;
the earlier serverless *script* task aborted with SIGABRT before reporting
anything. Run as a serverless *notebook* task instead, the same harness got
through, and the actual platform limits surfaced one by one (no code change;
the findings are recorded in the guide's Live verification).

Observed (2026-11-02, fevm-ontobricks-int, Lakebase `ontobricks-int`, scratch
objects dropped afterwards):
- Serverless compute refuses continuous streaming triggers:
  `[INFINITE_STREAMING_TRIGGER_NOT_SUPPORTED] Trigger type ProcessingTime is
  not supported for this cluster type. Use … AvailableNow, Once` — the precise
  reason Real-Time Mode (and any always-on stream) needs classic compute.
  The DBFS root is disabled there too (checkpoints go to a UC volume, as the
  RTM job already does with `ONTOBRICKS_REALTIME_CHECKPOINT_ROOT`); the
  runtime's bundled SDK predates the Lakebase API (the credential was minted
  by the submitter and passed as a parameter).
- With `trigger(availableNow=True)` over a Delta table of Debezium-style
  events (standing in for the topic), two waves on one checkpoint:
  wave 1, 40 upserts → 80 state rows and 80 live rows in the `_live` table
  (two slots per customer), 0 tombstones; wave 2, state restored from the
  checkpoint, 10 renames + 5 deletes → 70 live rows, 10 tombstones, 10 renamed
  labels, 0 stale labels, 0 deleted keys still live. The processor's state
  init / restore, slot diff, delete → tombstone and `seq` handling and the
  sink's guarded upserts behave on real Spark as the property tests say.
- Not measured: Real-Time-Mode latency (the design's p99 < 1 s) and a Kafka
  source — both need a classic DBR 19 cluster and a topic.

